      "A `DataFrame` is a table — rows and named columns, built on top of NumPy arrays underneath. `pd.read_csv(\"data.csv\")` loads a file in one line; `df.head()`, `df.info()`, and `df.describe()` are your first three commands on any new dataset, giving you a shape, types, and missing-value count before you do anything else. Learn `df[df[\"age\"] > 18]` for filtering, `df.groupby(\"category\").mean()` for aggregation, and `df.dropna()` / `df.fillna()` for handling missing data — a decision that quietly determines how good your downstream model can be. Merging datasets (`pd.merge`) and reshaping (`pivot_table`) round out the toolkit you'll reach for in almost every AI project's first hour.",
